## Why This Is Asked

This question probes how a candidate designs a privacy-conscious, multi-tenant ML pipeline on GCP, balancing data governance, per-tenant isolation, cost, and operability.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation with per-tenant datasets and Feature Store
- PII de-identification in Dataflow
- Canary deployments and traffic shifting
- Drift detection and Explainable AI for governance
- Privacy logging and rollback mechanisms

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: shift partial traffic to canary on drift alert
function rollbackOnDrift(isDrift, canaryEndpoint, prodEndpoint){
  if(!isDrift) return;
  // gradually shift traffic back to prod
  setTraffic(canaryEndpoint, 0.0);
  setTraffic(prodEndpoint, 1.0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle tenant onboarding/offboarding with data retention?
- How would you scale feature store lookups for thousands of tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a privacy-conscious, scalable fraud-detection pipeline across multiple tenants with governance and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Privacy-preserving aggregation (secure sum, differential privacy)
- Vertex AI Pipelines and canary rollout
- Dataflow enrichment, BigQuery provenance
- Data Catalog policy tagging
- Drift detection and explainability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: privacy budget check
function shouldRollout(latencyMs, drift, budget) {
  const okLatency = latencyMs < 250;
  const okDrift = drift < 0.1;
  const okBudget = budget.remaining >= budget.required;
  return okLatency && okDrift && okBudget;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-tenant feature stores in Vertex AI Feature Store?
- How would you simulate a canary rollout with rollbacks if fraud latency spikes?
